WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Super Mario Brothers, do you have what it takes to save the Mushroom Princess?

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.540
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.580
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.160
And I'm Joe. Hey, Joe.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.340
And just for anybody wondering, we'll have Sean and Sam on later, but I couldn't get them

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.400
all at the same time.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.900
So I got Joe coming first.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.000
We're busy people. Yeah.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.500
You know, this is every time.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.220
Well, you know, you were remote for a while.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.880
I was. I had a blanket fort to record.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.960
And now you're here. So I'm just happy to get you.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.360
And so that's why we're kind of doing these at different points.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.220
But that's not the point of today's episode.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.420
Today, we're talking about Super Mario Brothers, which is kind of funny because for the Famicom,

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.260
this is like a swan song before the Famicom disk system comes.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.980
But on the NES, it's like treated as, you know, it's a launch game.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.880
It's treated as like a big deal for the console to show off its superior power.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.940
Right. I mean, it was the I mean, it was the best thing out there at the time.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.280
Yeah. And so it's just funny how like one console is ending in Japan while the other one is

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.520
just starting. But I think that also shows the difference between the NES and the Famicom.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.340
I think a lot of people think of them as the same exact architecture.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.600
And they really weren't.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.240
The Famicom disk system offered a ton of improvements that the NES already had.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.480
If you know, we never really got an NES disk system over here.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:37.240
Right. Well, if I'm being totally honest, I'm actually not never been 100 percent clear on what the

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.440
difference is between the two consoles.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.000
So it's interesting. Yeah.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.100
And so just a brief thing that I know we talked about maybe on the first episode is that when

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.720
Nintendo was developing a console over here for North America, they went through a couple

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.800
different things and they were even going to work with Atari at one point to make the Nintendo

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.860
AVS, which was, I guess, like the advanced video system or something like that.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.620
And Atari was going to work on it with them.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.780
That deal fell through.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.200
And probably for good reason.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.880
I don't think Atari would have been the best partner for Nintendo.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:06.960
But now we have the NES.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.740
We have the 17 launch games.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.600
And here we are with Super Mario Brothers designed by Shigeru Miyamoto and Takahashi Tezuka.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.960
And I know Miyamoto.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.740
That's like a common name now, not only in this podcast, but in the world.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.300
Everybody knows him.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.900
And I feel like Tezuka doesn't get really that great of a rap.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.800
His name definitely doesn't have that recognition that Miyamoto does.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.180
But he's responsible for probably just as many things in Super Mario Brothers.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.300
And I think as we're about to kind of uncover here, he's really responsible for a lot of

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.480
the major parts of this game that I think people associated with it being so good.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.940
So just to give a little backstory here, Tezuka talks about the initial concept in an interview

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.520
with Iwata back in like 2004.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:56.880
And so he says the initial concept behind Super Mario was that we wanted a dynamic, athletic kind of game that would be set on land, sea and air.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.220
And that would feature a large character.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.660
Now, a large character.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:02.800
I know these things.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.000
He gets large, I guess, when he gets a mushroom.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:11.500
I'm not sure if these are just like bad translations, you know, like if they're just taking like the most generic word to describe these things.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.680
But I think it is kind of interesting to think about how the Japanese talk about these video games.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:25.440
Because, again, like, do you ever think of Mario is like we want a dynamic, athletic kind of game and the game should take place on land, sea and air?

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:26.560
Like, is that how you would?

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.260
I mean, I guess I can't deny that it does those things.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.720
I mean, athletic is kind of a weird word because we have sports games.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.460
But but it makes sense.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.400
I mean, it's definitely he's definitely an athletic plumber.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:46.600
And I think like what's also interesting here is because they're talking about the land, sea and air is that, you know, Super Mario Brothers kind of and I'm not I'm not going to go out there and say they

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.400
invented the platformer.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.420
There were kind of games that were already like that.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.060
I mean, Pitfall comes to mind on the Atari 2600.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.340
I don't think that's a platformer game.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.100
I think it's more of like action adventure, but it definitely has like similar structure to like a Super Mario Brother game.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:07.960
But Super Mario Brothers really like changes everything about the platformer genre and just about video games in general.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:13.220
Definitely. And the only thing that confuses me a little bit about that is the the air aspect of it.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.760
Because I don't know that Super Mario Bros, the original, didn't have a lot.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:25.800
Yeah. I can see them thinking about the air and just like, you know, in terms of space, like the jumps and the leaps where there's no ground.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.300
But, you know, you're kind of like that makes sense.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.580
World one three. I know you're on like those mushrooms.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.800
Yeah, I guess that's true. You are kind of in the sky there.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:39.280
I'm thinking more of like later Mario games where you can actually like fly or you're in the clouds or it's like more aesthetically air.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.260
Right. Yeah. And who knows?

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:53.300
I mean, I can only wonder how many things they wanted to do in this game that's already pushing the limitations of the Famicom, not necessarily the NES, but definitely pushing the limitations of the Famicom to its brain.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:59.940
And they probably wanted to do so much more that they couldn't really get around until, you know, Super Mario Brothers two or Super Mario Brothers three.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.780
Right. So Super Mario Brothers started off, Tezuka mentions, as a large character.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.400
It was really just a 16 by 32 pixel square.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:10.740
OK, that's how they were designing the game.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:14.960
Like you mean Mario was Mario was a square first, like not even a character.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:16.140
But this is for the prototype.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.780
This is for the prototype. But they're just designing the levels.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.980
They're really focusing on that aspect of it.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:27.160
Mario only became the main character in this game once Tezuka saw the sale figures for Mario Brothers.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.140
So this game never had to be a Mario game.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.160
It wasn't even supposed to be Mario the character at all.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.060
The character at all.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.300
This was not a this was not decided that it was going to be Super Mario Brothers.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.600
And it was it was Mario Brothers that did it, not Donkey Kong.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:40.760
So that's the interesting point.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.760
And something that I thought about, too, is why would it be Mario Brothers?

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.600
And here's what Tezuka has to say about it.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.100
This is another quote from Tezuka.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.940
Well, at lunchtime, I'd often wander to various offices and chat to the people from other departments.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.140
So I'd become friendly with people that way.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:06:02.560
Anyway, I was shown the sales figures and I saw that although Mario Brothers on the Famicom had been released over a year previously,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.460
it was still selling consistently well.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:12.180
It became at that point where I said, put Mario into the game and it'll boost the sales figures.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:18.920
So interesting that Donkey Kong is definitely responsible for Mario Brothers in that sense, you know, like developing those characters.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:27.920
But it's another instance of an NES game that has Mario included in it only after the fact, not really designed with Mario in mind.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:35.980
Because I don't know if you had a similar experience, but growing up, you know, after all of this, I, you know, I always knew that Super Mario Brothers Mario came from Donkey Kong.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:40.040
But it wasn't until I was a lot older that I had even heard of just the original Mario Brothers.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:46.920
Yeah, it seems for me, the canon is also weird because I think we are, we're not 80s kids.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.520
We don't have this arcade background.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.300
So Donkey Kong isn't even really that important to us.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.580
I would say Super Mario Brothers is like a lot more important.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:58.500
And so for me, I always thought Super Mario Brothers is like the beginning.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:05.380
You know, I don't even think about Mario Brothers or Wrecking Crew or Donkey Kong, Donkey Kong Jr.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.820
Donkey Kong Jr. is where we get the Mario name.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:09.740
In Donkey Kong, he's just Jumpman.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.500
So there's all these important games that come before it.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.460
But I think everyone kind of sees Super Mario Brothers as...

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.720
Right. Because that's the first one where the gameplay feels like what we now know as Mario.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:19.960
What we now know as Mario, right.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:26.000
I think it would almost be interesting to see what a Mario Brothers sequel would be without Super in it.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.040
You know, like a sewer based Brooklyn game.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.500
Well, that's kind of what we get in Super Mario Brothers 3.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.640
There's Mario Brothers levels built into it.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:43.700
Oh, yeah. I don't know if you call it an engine on NES, but they use the engine of the Super Mario Brothers 3 game.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.700
Yeah, they definitely already had the programming there anyway.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.420
So yeah, that's a good point to bring that up.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:08:02.580
So just one more thing that I think is kind of surprising besides the fact that Mario was added to the game late is that the idea of Super Mario and calling it Super Mario Brothers only happened when the Super Mushroom was added to the game.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:13.100
Originally, you were just going to be the large character and then they developed the Super Mushroom to kind of have like a, you know, a life system within the life system.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:14.660
So it wasn't like an instant death.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:19.120
Right. And then they said, oh, well, you know, if it's called the Super Mushroom, make it the Super Mario Brothers.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:25.500
And I think it's funny, even brothers, you know, getting added to this is like almost each part of this title has a story.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:32.740
Right. Super from Super Mushroom, Mario because of the sales of Mario Brothers and brothers because they had the two player.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:42.280
All right. And if this beginning of this episode wasn't filled with enough surprises, they did reveal kind of around the time I think Super Mario Maker was coming out.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:45.500
They started talking about different control schemes that they had originally planned.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:55.800
And one of the original control schemes for Super Mario Brothers involves up being mad being mapped to or sorry, jump being mapped to up.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:00.460
Right. Instead of the A button, which I think is crazy and would have completely crazy now.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.860
But I understand why that was their thought process.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:04.140
Yeah, it would be terrible.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:08.580
Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. It would be terrible just from a how much you're using your left and right already.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:12.620
And then to have to like fidget your thumb to push the up button.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:26.720
I think it would have been a totally it would have been a a more finite experience of the game because you wouldn't be able to do all of the like loose controlling of you're running on one side on one thumb and you're pressing the buttons on the other.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:39.960
Right. But here's the real twist is that A was mapped to attack, attack, attack, like you would actually be like before they had ever discovered like jumping on the enemies would be a good idea.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.520
They were planning to have it like power ups that you could use to attack.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.860
So if you had just your bare hands, you would attack with your fist.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.880
Oh, like you'd have to close combat.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.140
Yeah, like close combat.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.040
If you had those Goombas, you wouldn't be able to jump on them.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:54.980
You'd have to attack and punch them.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:00.380
And then if you got the rifle, it acted as like this is this is crazy.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:04.440
I'm bringing it up so casually, but this was a plan before they ever thought.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.500
So that'd just be like the fire flower or I guess it would.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:08.840
I mean, I don't think it would.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.120
Are we talking like we're talking Call of Duty here?

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.940
Yeah. All of a sudden you enter like a first person mode.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:21.000
A bit mode. I think that's just crazy that they were thinking about causing an attack instead of that would be a.

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:22.100
Totally different game.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:26.140
I mean. Yeah. I mean, I would also like think about how Mario would be different today.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:27.720
Yeah, he'd be gritty.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:33.220
They kind of they put attacks into the later games like in Super Mario 64.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:34.820
There is a punch.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:36.200
But who uses that?

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.260
I didn't know there was.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.340
I default to jumping on things because that's how I know Mario.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:52.060
And in Galaxy, there's like the spin where if you flick the we the we mode and the nunchuck, it's like a spin that he can do that'll instantly attack anyone.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:53.080
And that's like a little cheap.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:58.280
But again, like something that like anyone who's played Mario games doesn't resort to that.

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:02.720
Really, you're always thinking first, like I got to jump on this thing like that's Mario's thing.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:10.940
But I think what's important there is to think about how they once they got rid of a to attack and made the game about jumping on the enemies.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.460
That's really showing how they started to focus so much on the jump.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.900
And I think that's what I want to talk about now is kind of like the movement and gameplay.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:24.580
Right. Well, I mean, the gameplay, the movement for me is like it's pretty, pretty close to perfect for that time.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:33.900
Like, I mean, as far as like the weight that Mario has and the physics compared to other games of that time, like jumping and moving feels so much more realistic.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.240
As silly as that sounds.

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:38.980
I think the only thing similar in our launch games is Ice Climber and Kung Fu.

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.080
Right. And both we've talked about already.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.760
And Ice Climber, we definitely had some problems with the with the movement.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.780
Ice Climber's movement, especially the jump, is poor.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:53.500
And I think with Kung Fu, while we like the ability to go forward and back and not have that kind of restriction,

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:58.380
I think that had a huge problem with its jump as well and also its movement options.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:02.280
Like the jump for Kung Fu for that game is fine.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.760
It's OK in that game.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:11.780
But you could never program that jump into a Mario game and have it be fun because there's too much fast paced, you know, platforming that you have to do.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:14.200
That's, you know, Mario having.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.020
I don't remember when it was explained to me as weight.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:20.980
But when I heard that, I was like, that makes the most sense because you land on the ground.

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:25.400
He kind of has a little momentum and it makes it feel a little more real.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:26.740
I think it's all about the momentum.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:37.760
But I think another really cool part about the jump is that just depending on how long you've been running and how hard you hold your jump button, you can jump so many.

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.500
There's so many different options to jump.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:42.780
It's not like, you know, there is a limit to how far you can jump.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:48.720
But in between the max that you can jump and the minimum amount you can jump, there are so many options.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:57.380
And I think that's where Mario really shines is that like with Ice Climber, once you hit the jump button, you're forced to go all the way up and wait to come back down.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.580
With Mario, you can just go as small as like two tiles over or as many as eight.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:06.120
And that must have to do with just how long you're holding the button, right?

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:10.580
Yeah, but also as well as how long you're holding the run because it's like an arc.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:18.920
So really, if you think about it, I mean, like I know that the number of possible jumps is limited to a certain amount, but it's actually a ridiculously high number for an NES game.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:24.180
There's a lot of finesse involved in those two buttons, you know, that you're using to control it.

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:30.320
And I mean, I know that we're really talking hard about this jump right now, but I mean, you would describe that as the game, no?

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:32.360
Yeah, exactly. It's a jumping simulator.

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:38.320
I mean, yeah, there's a couple of, you know, you got the fire flower and you got the, you know, I guess, and the star, I guess.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:40.940
Yeah, the star is like something I, you know, that's hard to find.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.100
It's like the one-up mushroom. You don't see those as often.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.960
They're prolific, you know, but they're really not found that often.

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:55.960
All right, so real fast, before we go any further, let's rewind time for a little bit because we know this isn't your first time playing the game like some of our other NES games.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:01.680
So tell me about your earliest Super Mario Brothers experience.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:13.260
So it's tough for me to actually pinpoint my earliest experience with this game because it's just that game that's always kind of been there in the, you know, in my subconscious, I guess.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:15.260
You know, it's always, you know, I had an NES growing up.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:22.460
I've always had this game and it was always overshadowed because I had Super Mario Brothers 3 as well.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:24.540
Yeah, having that knowledge ahead of you.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:29.640
Right. Yeah. So, I mean, it was one that I would always go play, but then I would usually like play Super Mario Brothers 3.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:40.260
So, you know, I obviously, you know, I've played it a lot when I was a kid, but I don't think I ever played through and beat it when I was a kid because I always had another option that I preferred.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:44.820
Right. When you played it as a kid, did it feel old to you?

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.040
No, but there's a reason for that with a lot of the games that I played on the NES.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:54.100
Listen, that's just because, I mean, I was born in 1990, so.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:55.620
You're NES material.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:56.700
I mean, you know. Right, right.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:03.660
But when I, once I was older, once I was, you know, old enough to start playing it, Super Nintendo was out and 64 was even out eventually.

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:07.340
But, you know, I didn't realize that I was playing an outdated machine.

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:09.460
I just thought that these were video games and they were awesome.

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:16.160
And I, you know, and I would go to, you know, a yard sale and buy a box of video games for like 50 cents.

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:17.620
And I just thought that's what video games were.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:18.960
Or things you could buy for 50 cents.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.120
So none of them really felt old to me.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:27.140
